            <div xml:id="d641">

                <pb facs="0886" n="772" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg772"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">738.3915/423: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-05-31T17:00:00-04:40">May 31, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>35. Your telegram of May 29, 2 p.m. Stafford, just returned from Santiago,
                    reports President said he had given no orders to clear the region of foreign
                    troops and had no intention of precipitating a conflict with the Haitians. He
                    showed excerpts of note from Haitian Minister <pb facs="0887" n="773"
                        xml:id="pg773"/> here complaining of persecution by Dominican soldiers in
                    territory always recognized as Haitian territory, and also telegrams from
                    Dominican Chargé d’Affaires at Port-au-Prince stating that the Haitian
                    Government wished to create border incidents in order to distract attention from
                    internal conditions.</p>
                <p>Full report by air mail due in Washington Thursday morning.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Curtis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
